WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.632
Curl wants the home page of example.com.

2
00:00:05.632 --> 00:00:10.543
It writes a request of 74 bytes: a GET line and three header lines.

3
00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:13.602
The kernel carries those bytes down four layers.

4
00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:15.964
Each layer puts its own header in front.

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:24.558
TCP adds 32 bytes: the two ports, which pick the program at each end, and numbers that keep the bytes in order.

6
00:00:24.558 --> 00:00:26.825
Now it's 106 bytes, a segment.

7
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:34.387
IP adds 20 bytes: the two IP addresses, which pick the machine, and a time to live counter.

8
00:00:34.387 --> 00:00:36.327
Now 126 bytes, a packet.

9
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:44.553
Ethernet adds 14: the two MAC addresses, which pick the network card on this one link, and a type field.

10
00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:46.019
140 bytes, a frame.

11
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:47.684
This is encapsulation.

12
00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:52.433
Each layer wraps what the layer above handed it, and never looks inside.

13
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:58.507
The destination MAC is the router's, the next card on your link.

14
00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:01.555
So the frame crosses one cable, to the router.

15
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:10.413
The router strips the Ethernet header, reads the destination IP address, and lowers the time to live from 64 to 63.

16
00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:15.250
Then it wraps the packet in a new Ethernet header for the next link.

17
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.559
MAC addresses change at every hop.

18
00:01:17.559 --> 00:01:19.528
IP addresses and ports don't.

19
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.755
At the server, the kernel runs the same steps backwards.

20
00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:25.893
Each header says what is inside it.

21
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.575
The Ethernet type field says an IP packet follows.

22
00:01:29.575 --> 00:01:31.660
Off comes the Ethernet header.

23
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:36.366
The IP protocol number, 6, says a TCP segment follows.

24
00:01:36.366 --> 00:01:38.365
Off comes the IP header.

25
00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:41.776
The TCP port, 80, picks the web server.

26
00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:45.151
It reads exactly the 74 bytes curl wrote.

27
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:48.061
On the way down, each layer adds a header.

28
00:01:48.061 --> 00:01:51.333
On the way up, each layer reads one and removes it.

29
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:53.707
Routers touch only the outermost one.
